    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, that's much clearer, thanks. So if the pressure difference is the main thing, how does Newton's third law factor into this? I've heard people say that lift is also about the wing deflecting air downwards. How do these two ideas—the pressure difference from Bernoulli and the downward deflection from Newton—actually work together instead of contradicting each other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're absolutely right to consider both Bernoulli's principle and Newton's third law when discussing lift. They are complementary rather than contradictory. Bernoulli's principle explains the pressure difference across the wing, while Newton's third law, which states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction, explains the force exerted on the wing by the air.\n\nWhen air flows over the wing, it is deflected downwards due to the wing's shape and angle of attack. This downward deflection of air is the action. According to Newton's third law, the reaction to this action is an upward force exerted by the wing on the air. This upward force is what we perceive as lift.\n\nThe pressure difference created by Bernoulli's principle contributes to the overall lift by reducing the pressure above the wing, which, when combined with the downward deflection of air, results in a net upward force. The faster air moving over the top of the wing (Bernoulli's principle) and the downward deflection of air (Newton's third law) work together to create the lift that keeps the aircraft aloft. Both principles are essential for understanding the full picture of how lift is generated in flight.",
      "source": "target",
